\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Neural Ordinary Differential Equations}

Neural Ordinary Differential Equations (Neural ODEs) were introduced by \cite{chen2018neural} to address the challenge of learning continuous-time dynamics from discrete observations. This approach enables the modeling of continuous systems using discrete data, a capability that is particularly valuable in fields such as biology, physics, and finance. The core concept involves defining a neural network that represents the derivative of a latent state variable with respect to time. By integrating this network, one can predict the system's state at future time points.

\subsection{Meta-Learning}

Meta-learning, or "learning to learn," aims to train models that can rapidly adapt to new tasks with minimal data. This paradigm has gained significant traction in recent years, with applications spanning computer vision, natural language processing, and reinforcement learning. A prominent example is the MAML algorithm \cite{finn2017model}, which optimizes initial parameters to facilitate fast adaptation. Another key approach is the meta-learner, which learns a function to generate task-specific models. This work adopts the meta-learner framework, employing an ODE-based neural network to predict the next state of a dynamical system.

