\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
This paper addresses the task of determining the most probable word sequence for a given sentence. The problem is framed as a generative process, where the objective is to identify the sequence that maximizes the probability assigned by a model. The model is trained to predict the probability distribution of the next word in a sequence based on the preceding words. The study utilizes the Penn Treebank dataset \cite{Marcus1993} and compares the proposed method against the baseline from \cite{Ferrara2019} and the state-of-the-art approach from \cite{Ferrara2019}. The results show that the proposed method outperforms the baseline and matches the performance of the state-of-the-art model, while also achieving a lower perplexity score. The code is accessible at \url{https://github.com/robertoferrara/word_sequence_generation}.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
The problem of generating the most probable word sequence is a well-established challenge in natural language processing. A common strategy involves training a model to predict the probability distribution of the next word given the previous ones. This approach is widely used in applications such as text generation and machine translation. The model is typically trained on a large corpus of text, where the goal is to learn the underlying distribution of words in the language. Once trained, the model can be used to generate new text by predicting the next word in a sequence. This process is repeated until a complete sentence or paragraph is generated.

